      "value": "@{5e44a1bd0c91c2477a8b8acb|full_name}: If you upload something with the blend shape set to 40, it should look like set to 40 in game unless an animation is triggered that increases or decreases it.  That was the behavior before 1088 and indeed it still works for all of my blend shapes that do NOT have an animation somewhere that changes them.  The bug is that just having the animation present on the FX layer is causing the game to get confused on what the correct default state of the blend shape should be (40) and for some reason it is being rendered as a much larger number - regardless of whether that animation is triggered or not.  I don't know exactly why it happens.  It is definitely a problem affecting more than just the one avatar I am using though, and other people are seeing it on some of their avatars too based on discussions in the VRchat discord.  It is behavior that did NOT happen before version 1088.\nIf you are interested in reproducing the issue, based on the many, many hours I have spent trying to figure out why this is happening you would need to make sure the following bare minimum conditions are met:\n1. Set an obvious blendshape to something like 40.  In the case of my avatar, the pupil L and R ones.\n2. Make sure your avatar has an fx controller and a gesture controller assigned.  The gesture controller can even be blank with just the first layer, it doesn't actually matter what is on that controller- but this bug does not happen if there is no Gesture controller assigned at all, so there has to be one assigned in addition to there being an FX controller.  On my avatar there is only an FX and Gesture controller, no others (such as locomotion, action, etc).  Just those two.\n3. Create an animation that sets those same blendshapes to 100.\n4. On the FX controller, make sure GestureLeft is a defined parameter INT and then create a new layer.  Create new state (orange) - you can call it Idle.  No animation assigned to it.  Then create a transition from Any state -> Idle with condition GestureLeft = 0 (not performing any gesture).  Finally create a new state and set the animation you created earlier on it.  Create a transition from Any state -> the new state you created with condition GestureLeft = 4 (peace sign).  You can also make GestureLeft = 20, it doesn't matter - even if this condition is impossible to reach, the bug will still be triggered.\nYou can see from the screenshots below that my pupils in game are HUGE compared to how they are in unity, even though there is no possible way the animation that affects that blend shape could be getting triggered.  I made the condition for that transition GestureLeft = 20, which it cannot possibly ever reach because VRchat will only set it from 0-7 based on the different hand gestures.  Indeed, if I turn on debug I can see that GestureLeft is set to 0 and that the layer in FX controller is in the Idle state.  So the animation is not being triggered according to the game, but somehow just having it existing there is screwing up what the game perceives as the default blendshape state.  If I delete that particular animation, then the problem does not happen.  Based on all of that, this seems to clearly be a bug - this started with 1088.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
